<body>
  <header>
    <a class="brand" href="./">Halcyon Threshold</a>
    <div class="version">Incident Readiness Kit · v0.1 · 16 November 2026</div>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="title">
      <div class="eyebrow">A small public good, built to be tested</div>
      <h1 id="title">Better incident reports.<br>Under time pressure.</h1>
      <p class="lede">A structured report format, synthetic tabletop exercises, and an evaluation protocol for teams developing or deploying advanced AI.</p>
      <div class="status"><strong>Experimental and unvalidated.</strong> No field study has been run. This is not a regulatory standard, safety certification, emergency service, or substitute for legal advice.</div>
      <div class="buttons">
        <a class="button primary" href="README.md">Start the 60-minute rehearsal</a>
        <a class="button" href="schema/incident-report.schema.json">Get the JSON Schema</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="questions-title">
      <div class="eyebrow">Decision-critical clarity</div>
      <h2 id="questions-title">Four questions a first report should answer</h2>
      <ol class="questions">
        <li>What happened, and how do we know?</li>
        <li>What has happened versus what could still happen?</li>
        <li>Is the event contained, and who owns the next decision?</li>
        <li>What remains unknown, and when will the report be updated?</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="kit-title">
      <div class="eyebrow">Use the pieces separately or together</div>
      <h2 id="kit-title">Inside the kit</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>Report and triage</h3><p>A 12-minute human template, triage prompts, and a versioned machine-readable format. Unknowns and evidence gaps stay visible.</p><a href="templates/initial-report.md">Initial report</a> · <a href="templates/triage.md">Triage card</a></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Rehearse safely</h3><p>Four entirely fictional scenarios with facilitator injects. No production access, real incident details, or sensitive data needed.</p><a href="exercises/facilitator-guide.md">Facilitator guide</a> · <a href="exercises/participant-scenarios.md">Scenarios</a></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Measure the tool</h3><p>A counterbalanced pilot, blinded scoring rubric, consent materials, and pre-specified null and stop criteria.</p><a href="evaluation/protocol.md">Evaluation protocol</a> · <a href="evaluation/rubric.md">Scoring rubric</a></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="split" aria-labelledby="run-title">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">A facilitator and 2–4 participants are enough</div>
        <h2 id="run-title">Run a first rehearsal</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Choose a fictional scenario and assign incident-response roles.</li>
          <li>Set a 12-minute timer and complete an initial report. Escalate without waiting for a perfect form.</li>
          <li>Reveal new information at minutes 4 and 8. Record decisions and timestamps.</li>
          <li>Freeze and score the initial report independently.</li>
          <li>Identify the omissions most likely to impair a decision and assign one process change.</li>
        </ol>
        <a href="README.md">Read the complete quick start →</a>
      </div>
      <aside class="card">
        <h3>What a good score does not mean</h3>
        <p>A polished report does not show that a system is aligned, safe, secure, or compliant. The first evaluation measures report completeness, time, unsupported claims, and usability—not reductions in real-world harm.</p>
        <a href="sources.md">Read sources and limitations</a>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contribute-title">
      <div class="eyebrow">Criticism before promotion</div>
      <h2 id="contribute-title">Help find what is wrong with it</h2>
      <p>The most useful contribution is a specific ambiguity, unsafe instruction, scoring problem, or independent test. Null and negative results belong in the record. Do not send confidential incident details or personal data.</p>
      <p><a href="feedback.md">Review prompts</a> · <a href="examples/initial-report.json">Fictional example report</a> · <a href="mailto:morgan@halcyonthreshold.org?subject=Incident%20Readiness%20Kit%20feedback">Email project feedback</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer>
    <p><strong>Halcyon Threshold</strong> is an independent public-interest research and engineering project working toward the best possible outcome for the arrival of superintelligent AI.</p>
